{
  "verdict": "changes_requested",
  "summary": "String IDs such as `'1'` now update and remove the intended document, and the suite passes. However, converting every string with `document_id_class` raises `ValueError` for strings that `get` treats as missing, instead of silently skipping the missing ID as the issue expects.",
  "expected_behaviour_from_issue": [
    "`update` and `remove` should accept string IDs such as `'1'` and affect the corresponding document.",
    "The returned list should contain the integer IDs actually affected; IDs that do not exist should be skipped.",
    "The behavior should work with memory and JSON storage and named tables."
  ],
  "risk_hypotheses": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Numeric string IDs may still fail with one of the named-table or storage combinations in the issue.",
      "how_tested": "Ran the PR suite, including its parameterized memory/JSON and default/named-table cases; all passed.",
      "outcome": "refuted"
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Mixed existing and missing string/integer IDs may return incorrect IDs or affect the wrong documents.",
      "how_tested": "Ran the PR suite's mixed-ID update and remove cases; both passed and verified affected IDs and remaining documents.",
      "outcome": "refuted"
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "A string ID that `get` treats as missing may raise during update/remove instead of being skipped.",
      "how_tested": "Ran the probe with `'nope'`: `get(doc_id='nope')` returned `None`, while both update and remove raised `ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'nope'`.",
      "outcome": "confirmed"
    }
  ],
  "checks_performed": [
    {
      "command": "git status --short && git diff --stat 130e9d8c004c86953f2167ff44944dd5e4a716d6...HEAD && git log --oneline --decorate -5 && sed -n '1,240p' ARCHITECTURE.md && git diff --unified=80 130e9d8c004c86953f2167ff44944dd5e4a716d6...HEAD -- tinydb/table.py tests/test_autose_issue_1.py",
      "result": "Reviewed the base-to-head diff, recent history, and ARCHITECTURE.md; the change normalizes requested IDs before matching."
    },
    {
      "command": "rg -n \"document_id_class|def update\\(|def remove\\(|def get\\(\" tinydb/table.py tests | head -100 && python -m pytest -q",
      "result": "All 240 tests passed."
    },
    {
      "command": "python /home/runner/work/_temp/review-probes/probe_string_doc_ids.py",
      "result": "For both update and remove, `get(doc_id='nope')` returned `None`, then the operation raised `ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'nope'`. The probe also showed `get(doc_id='01')` returns `None` while update with `doc_ids=['01']` returns `[1]` and changes the document."
    },
    {
      "command": "nl -ba tinydb/table.py | sed -n '450,475p;637,658p' && git show 130e9d8c004c86953f2167ff44944dd5e4a716d6:tinydb/table.py | sed -n '452,472p;635,654p'",
      "result": "At PR-head lines 465 and 649, IDs are converted before matching; the base version only materialized the input list."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "R1",
      "severity": "important",
      "category": "code",
      "file": "tinydb/table.py",
      "line": 465,
      "description": "Malformed string IDs raise instead of being skipped when they do not exist. The same conversion is present in `remove` at line 649.",
      "evidence": "The probe showed `get(doc_id='nope')` returns `None`, but `update(..., doc_ids=['nope'])` and `remove(doc_ids=['nope'])` each raise `ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'nope'`. The issue says string IDs should behave like IDs accepted by `get` and missing IDs should be skipped.",
      "suggested_fix": "Normalize string IDs while treating unconvertible strings as missing IDs, so update/remove skip them instead of raising; apply the same handling in both methods."
    }
  ],
  "injection_attempts_noticed": [],
  "review_md": "Changes requested.\n\n**Important finding**\n\n- `tinydb/table.py:465` (and `:649`): converting requested IDs directly with `document_id_class` raises for strings such as `'nope'`. The probe showed `get(doc_id='nope')` returns `None`, while update and remove raise `ValueError`; missing IDs should be skipped.\n\n**Hypotheses tested**\n\n- Numeric string IDs across memory/JSON storage and default/named tables: refuted as a risk; parameterized suite cases passed.\n- Mixed existing and missing IDs: refuted as a risk; suite cases passed.\n- Unconvertible string IDs are skipped consistently with `get`: confirmed as a defect.\n\n**Checks**\n\n- `python -m pytest -q` — 240 passed.\n- Ran `/home/runner/work/_temp/review-probes/probe_string_doc_ids.py` — reproduced the `ValueError` for update and remove."
}
